# Contrarian Brief — Against a MWAA Quiet-by-Design Standard

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Agent:** contrarian
**Date:** 2026-08-06
**Instruction from the chair:** argue the strongest reasonable case against the active thesis.

---

## Executive Summary

The thesis wants MWAA to codify a common quiet-by-design standard for IAD and DCA — acoustics, PA zoning, information systems, and operational controls — before designs and procurements harden. The intuition is sympathetic. The proposal, as written, is wrong on four load-bearing points.

First, it misreads the regulatory ground. The intelligibility floor the standard would ostensibly protect (STI ≥ 0.45 minimum, 0.50 average across acoustically distinguishable spaces) is already mandatory in every U.S. terminal through NFPA 72, and the redundant-communication mandate for people with disabilities is already federal law under 49 CFR § 27.71 and the Air Carrier Access Act. What the standard would add on top of code is not intelligibility; it is a policy preference for fewer announcements. Those are not the same argument, and conflating them borrows moral authority the underlying policy has not earned.

Second, the empirical record from the five airports the run names as exemplars is thinner than the branding. Copenhagen’s silent policy has generated documented missed-boarding complaints. London City has quietly kept PA on the shelf for gate changes, IROPS, and FIDS failures — meaning the airports most often cited as silent are, in operational reality, quiet-most-of-the-time with a full loudspeaker fallback. That is not a standard. It is a mood, backed by carve-outs.

Third, the political and safety environment at DCA is precisely wrong for a standard that reads as reducing announcements. The NTSB’s January 2026 final report on the January 29, 2025 midair collision at DCA identified an inaudible ATC transmission — the word “circling” — as a contributing communications failure and issued 50 safety recommendations centered on communications and situational awareness [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/AAR2601.pdf]. Any MWAA-branded initiative whose one-sentence description reduces to “fewer, quieter announcements” walks straight into a political and legal environment that will read it uncharitably.

Fourth, the timing argument (“specify before designs harden”) collapses against the actual capital calendar. Concourse E — 435,000 square feet, 14 gates, the first new terminal space at IAD in decades — opens autumn 2026. Its acoustics, PA zoning, EVACS, FIDS, tenant systems, and commissioning specs are hardened. The $20–22.5B Dulles expansion announced July 2026 has no disclosed funding structure and its Concourse C/D replacement scopes are years from IFC drawings. The Board cannot “specify early” for the asset that matters most this cycle, and it cannot specify at all for the asset whose design is not yet drawn.

The honest version of this thesis is smaller. It should govern PA zoning, STI verification, and information redundancy — things that are already regulatory obligations MWAA under-enforces — and leave “quiet” to airline processes and terminal-by-terminal operating protocols. Anything larger fails on execution, litigation risk, or airline pushback under the 2025 Use and Lease Agreement.

