## 1. The Strongest Counter-Case — Seven Bullets

1. **The regulatory floor is already there. The standard would mostly restate it.** NFPA 72 (Chapter 24) requires intelligible voice notification with STI thresholds explicitly quantified. 49 CFR § 27.71 requires audible and visual communication redundancy at every DOT-assisted airport terminal. ADA and the Air Carrier Access Act require effective communication for deaf, deaf-blind, hard-of-hearing, and blind travelers. A MWAA standard that duplicates these requirements adds governance load without adding safety. A standard that goes beyond them — fewer announcements, tighter zoning, muted media — introduces conflict with the code floor, not compliance with it.
2. **The peer evidence is thin, branded, and non-transferable.** Schiphol calls itself “Silent” but continues to face public criticism for the volume of ambient chaos in its terminals. Copenhagen’s silent policy has produced documented passenger complaints about missed flights because the airport does not call passengers to gate. London City has been on a silent-terminal policy since 2008 — 18 years — and has carved out mandatory PA for gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure. None of the five named peers operate under FAA Part 139 + TSA layered rules or a signatory-airline Use and Lease Agreement. The regulatory transfer is not automatic; the operational transfer is smaller than the branding.
3. **The DCA political environment makes the timing wrong.** The NTSB’s January 2026 final report on the January 29, 2025 midair collision — 67 dead — identified an inaudible ATC transmission as a contributing factor and issued 50 recommendations largely focused on communications and situational awareness. A MWAA standard whose headline reads “fewer, quieter announcements” in the year after that finding will be litigated in the local press and possibly in court by disability advocates. The reputational asymmetry is severe.
4. **The airline governance channel is signatory, not sovereign.** The new 15-year Use and Lease Agreement took effect January 1, 2025. United (dominant at IAD) and American (dominant at DCA) hold MII leverage. Announcement governance, PA cadence, tenant technology standards, and commercial media constraints touch airline and concessionaire operations directly. MWAA cannot unilaterally mandate a quiet standard against the largest signatory carriers; it must negotiate. That is a multi-year cycle, and the leverage tilts toward the carriers now that residual-style rate-setting is gone.
5. **The timing argument is broken by the capital calendar.** Concourse E opens autumn 2026 — its acoustics and systems are commissioned, not specifiable. The DCA Terminal 2 refresh (~$63.9M) is in the field. The July 2026 Dulles expansion is a White House announcement with an open funding structure; the C/D replacement designs will not reach IFC for years, and their standards will be negotiated inside a new program budget the Board has not yet approved. There is no near-term project the standard actually catches at the correct governance moment.
6. **The historic-preservation envelope narrows the standard to new-build only.** IAD Main Terminal (Saarinen, 1962) is a National Historic Landmark; DCA Terminal 1 (Goodman, 1941) is on the National Register; DCA Terminal 2 (Pelli, 1997) is high-volume vaulted space where reverberation control is architecturally constrained. Section 106 review governs federal-action modifications. A standard that applies uniformly to “new and substantially renovated” space cannot be enforced against MWAA’s two most identity-defining buildings without preservation clearances that Sarah Bronin’s Section 106 apparatus does not fast-track for acoustic hardware.
7. **The accessibility case cuts the other way.** The dominant advocacy positions from the Hearing Loss Association of America, National Association of the Deaf, National Disability Rights Network, and Paralyzed Veterans of America have historically pressed airports and airlines for *more* redundant communication — captioned announcements, visual paging boards, in-app alerts, and assistive listening. A framework that codifies “quiet” as a design goal, even with redundant channels, invites disparate-impact complaints under ACAA and DOT enforcement. The advocacy calculus is not neutral; it leans against operator-driven volume reduction.

