## 2. Evidence Section

### The regulatory floor is already binding

NFPA 72 Chapter 24 governs emergency communications systems in U.S. buildings and requires that “at least 90 percent of the measurement locations within each ADS have a measured STI of not less than 0.45 and an average STI of not less than 0.50” for compliance [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/]. STI thresholds and Acoustically Distinguishable Space definitions are enforced through adoption of NFPA 72 in local building codes (Fairfax, Loudoun, Arlington for MWAA’s facilities). Any MWAA-branded standard would be layered *on top of* an existing code requirement, not filling a gap.

49 CFR § 27.71 requires that “terminal facilities and services owned, leased, or operated on any basis by a recipient at a commercial service airport shall be readily accessible to and usable by individuals with disabilities,” including visual counterparts to audible information [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-A/part-27/subpart-B/section-27.71]. The Air Carrier Access Act extends effective-communication requirements to airline processes inside terminals [Source: https://www.nad.org/resources/transportation-and-travel/air-travel/air-carrier-access-act-acaa/].

### The exemplar airports show the model’s cracks

Copenhagen Airport (CPH), one of the run’s five explicit references, operates on a silent-airport basis. A user review on Trustpilot documents a passenger who arrived three hours before departure, was in a queue of ~300 at passport control, and missed a return flight to Edinburgh because “the airport doesn't put out calls” and there were no screens in the passport gate; the complaint took three weeks to receive a non-substantive response [Source: https://www.trustpilot.com/review/www.cph.dk]. This is not a systemic study, but it is documented operator behavior — CPH has been silent since the early 2010s and has not developed a robust mitigation for high-volume passport-control queues.

London City Airport (LCY) has operated a silent-terminal policy since 2008. LCY’s own policy page confirms the standard includes carve-outs for “gate changes at short notice, during disruption (when there are cancelled or delayed flights) and when there is a flight information display failure” [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. The advertised policy is quiet operations under normal conditions with full-loudspeaker fallback in exactly the conditions that produce the highest passenger anxiety — IROPS, weather, gate change bunching. That is not a policy so much as a normal-day preference.

Schiphol’s official position states that the airport has “virtually no announcements” and pushes information exclusively to FIDS and the app [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. Schiphol is also under sustained regulatory and political pressure on external noise and flight-cap issues [Source: https://www.euronews.com/2023/11/15/dutch-government-ditches-plan-to-reduce-flights-at-schiphol-airport-following-internationa]. It is a European state-model operator, not a U.S. FAA Part 139 hub with a signatory U&L. Regulatory transfer is not automatic and MWAA staff should not treat the term “silent airport” as a portable engineering standard.

### The DCA political environment is not neutral ground

The NTSB’s hearing on the January 29, 2025 midair collision at DCA identified — as one contributing factor — that ATC issued a call to the Black Hawk crew that the plane was “at 1,200 feet circling to runway 33,” and “the NTSB believes the word ‘circling’ was inaudible, possibly contributing to the pilots' confusion” [Source: https://www.npr.org/2026/01/27/nx-s1-5689091/ntsb-dca-midair-collision-black-hawk-helicopter]. The Board delivered 50 recommendations touching FAA, Army, DOD, and DOT — many of them communications-focused [Source: https://enotrans.org/article/ntsb-unveils-findings-probable-cause-and-recommendations-from-dca-mid-air-collision-investigation/]. That report is now the political frame inside which any MWAA communications-governance decision will be received. A standard that reduces even *terminal-side* announcements walks into a public-safety political environment where any inaudibility finding will be weaponized against MWAA.

### The airline governance channel is contractual, not sovereign

The 2025 MWAA Use and Lease Agreement runs 15 years, through 2039, and was adopted alongside a $9.0B joint capital plan [context packet]. The July 2026 White House / DOT / United / MWAA announcement expanded the Dulles program to $20–22.5B over roughly ten years with an unresolved funding structure [Source: https://parametric-architecture.com/trumps-22-5-billion-dulles-airport]. Under a signatory-carrier U&L, PA governance, tenant technology standards, and announcement-cadence rules that touch airline operations (boarding calls, IROPS communications, code-share coordination) are subject to negotiation, not unilateral operator rule.

### The accessibility bar is a floor, not a target

The Hearing Loss Association of America, Paralyzed Veterans of America, National Association of the Deaf, and National Disability Rights Network have jointly pressed airports and airlines for redundant communication — captioning, visual paging, in-app alerts [Source: https://www.hearingloss.org/post_topic/travel/]. A federal Airline Passengers with Disabilities Bill of Rights framework has been under active DOT rulemaking [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/airconsumer/disabilitybillofrights]. A quiet standard, however carefully specified, invites scrutiny under ACAA effective-communication requirements and gives advocacy groups a lever they will use.

