## 3. Scenarios Where the Thesis Is Wrong

**Scenario 1 — The Concourse E post-opening backlash.** Concourse E opens autumn 2026 with the acoustic and PA design already commissioned. A MWAA quiet-standard adopted in 2026–27 either (a) exempts Concourse E and undercuts the “common standard” claim, or (b) requires post-opening retrofit inside a brand-new asset that United has already invested in as its IAD hub expansion. Either outcome damages MWAA credibility.

**Scenario 2 — The IROPS morning at DCA.** A November 2026 nor’easter cascades through the DCA schedule. Under a quiet-terminal protocol, passengers rely on FIDS, in-app notifications, and localized gate-side announcements. Cell service in the Pelli-designed Terminal 2 vault is intermittent (metal-roof shielding); the app is a partial substitute at best. Missed connections rise. American Airlines Media Relations tells the *Washington Post* that MWAA’s quiet policy contributed to the operational meltdown. The Board is asked, in a public meeting, to justify a standard whose failure mode is passenger harm during exactly the events passengers most need clear direction.

**Scenario 3 — The ACAA complaint.** A traveler with low vision and mild hearing loss misses a boarding at IAD Concourse C because the audible page was suppressed under the new zoning standard and the visual FIDS in the concessions area is small and high-mounted. A complaint filed under 49 CFR Part 27 and the Air Carrier Access Act names both United and MWAA. The complaint is settled but the standard becomes a case study in ACAA training materials — the opposite of the reputational outcome MWAA seeks.

**Scenario 4 — The MII objection.** United and American, under their post-2025 U&L MII rights, refuse to consent to a capital-cost pass-through for terminal acoustics beyond what is required by NFPA 72 and ADA/ACAA. MWAA either absorbs the cost against operating revenue, dilutes the standard to code-minimum (in which case the standard adds no protection above baseline), or spends political capital in a U&L amendment cycle that could otherwise be spent on the $20–22.5B Dulles expansion approval.

**Scenario 5 — The Section 106 stall.** The standard is written to apply to “substantially renovated” space. A Terminal 1 (DCA) or Main Terminal (IAD) intervention triggers Section 106 review of any exterior-visible or character-defining acoustic hardware. The National Trust and the Cultural Landscape Foundation weigh in. What was intended to be a design-manual update becomes a preservation dispute inside two National Register properties.

